<exercises xml:id="ex-ByParts">
    <exercise>
        <p>
            Explain how we build the Integration by Parts formula, as well as what the purpose of this integration strategy is.
        </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <p>
            How do you choose options for <m>u</m> and <m>dv</m>? What are some good strategies to think about?
        </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <p>
            Let's say that you make a choice for <m>u</m> and <m>dv</m> and begin working through the Integration by Parts strategy. How can you tell if you've made a poor choice for your parts? Can you <em>always</em> tell?
        </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Integrate the following.
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\dint 3x\sin(x)\;dx</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>u \amp = 3x \amp v \amp = -\cos(x)</mrow>
                        <mrow>du \amp = 3\;dx\;\; \amp dv \amp = \sin(x)\;dx</mrow>
                    </md>
                    <md> 
                        <mrow>\int 3x\sin(x)\;dx  \amp = -3x\cos(x)+\int 3\cos(x)\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = -3x\cos(x) + 3\sin(x) + C</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\dint 5xe^x\;dx</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>u \amp = 5x \amp v \amp = e^x</mrow>
                        <mrow>du \amp = 5\;dx \amp dv \amp = e^x\;dx</mrow>
                    </md>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\int 5xe^x\;dx \amp = 5xe^x - \int 5e^x\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = 5xe^x - 5e^x + C</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\dint x^2e^{-x}\;dx</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>u \amp = x^2 \amp v \amp = -e^{-x}</mrow>
                        <mrow>du \amp = 2x\;dx \amp dv \amp e^{-x}\;dx</mrow>
                    </md>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\int x^2e^{-x}\;dx \amp = -x^2e^{-x} + \int 2xe^{-x}\;dx </mrow>
                    </md>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>u \amp = 2x \amp v \amp = -e^{-x}</mrow>
                        <mrow>du \amp = 2\;dx \amp dv \amp = e^{-x}\;dx</mrow>
                    </md>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\int x^2e^{-x}\;dx \amp = -x^2e^{-x} + \int 2xe^{-x}\;dx </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = -x^2e^{-x} -2xe^{-x} + \int 2e^{-x}\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = -x^2e^{-x} - 2xe^{-x} - 2e^{-x} + C</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\dint x^2\ln(x)\;dx</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>u \amp = \ln(x) \amp v \amp = \frac{x^3}{3}</mrow>
                        <mrow>du \amp = \frac{1}{x}\;dx \amp dv \amp x^2\;dx</mrow>
                    </md>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\int x^2\ln(x)\;dx \amp = \frac{x^3\ln(x)}{3} - \int \frac{x^2}{3}\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \frac{x^3\ln(x)}{3} - \frac{x^3}{9} + C</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\dint x^2\cos(x)\;dx</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>u \amp = x^2 \amp v \amp \sin(x)</mrow>
                        <mrow>du \amp = 2x\;dx \amp dv \amp = \cos(x)\;dx</mrow>
                    </md>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\int x^2\cos(x)\;dx \amp = x^2\sin(x) - \int 2x\sin(x)\;dx</mrow>
                    </md>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>u \amp = 2x \amp v \amp -\cos(x)</mrow>
                        <mrow>du \amp = 2\;dx \amp dv \amp \sin(x)\;dx</mrow>
                    </md>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\int x^2\cos(x)\;dx \amp = x^2\sin(x) - \int 2x\sin(x)\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = x^2\sin(x) + 2x\cos(x) -\int 2\cos(x)\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = x^2\sin(x) + 2x\cos(x) - 2\sin(x) + C</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\dint x^3e^{-x}\;dx</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>u \amp = x^3 \amp v \amp =-e^{-x}</mrow>
                        <mrow>du \amp = 3x^2\;dx \amp dv \amp e^{-x}\;dx</mrow>
                    </md>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\int x^3e^{-x}\;dx \amp = -x^3e^{-x} + 3\int x^2e^{-x}\;dx</mrow>
                    </md>
                    Note that we found a solution for <m>\dint x^2e^{-x}\;dx</m> earlier, and so we can use that here!
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\int x^3e^{-x}\;dx \amp = -x^3e^{-x} + 3\int x^2e^{-x}\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = -x^3e^{-x} + 3(-x^2e^{-x} - 2x e^{-x} - 2e^{-x})+C</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = -x^3e^{-x} -3x^2e^{-x} -6x e^{-x} -6e^{-x} + C</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\dint x\sin(x)\cos(x)\;dx</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow> u \amp = x \amp v \amp = \frac{\sin^2(x)}{2}</mrow>
                        <mrow>du \amp = dx \amp dv \amp = \sin(x)\cos(x)\;dx</mrow>
                    </md>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\int x\sin(x)\cos(x)\;dx \amp = \frac{x\sin^2(x)}{2} - \frac{1}{2}\int \sin^2(x)\;dx </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \frac{\sin^2(x)}{2} - \frac{1}{4}\int 1-\cos(2x)\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \frac{\sin^2(x)}{2} - \frac{1}{4} x - \frac{\sin(2x)}{8} + C</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\dint e^x\sin(x)\;dx</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>u \amp = \sin(x) \amp v \amp = e^x</mrow>
                        <mrow>du \amp = \cos(x)\;dx \amp dv \amp = e^x \; dx</mrow>
                    </md>
                    <md>
                        \int e^x\sin(x) \;dx = e^x\sin(x) - \int e^x\cos(x)\;dx
                    </md>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>u \amp = \cos(x) \amp v \amp = e^x</mrow>
                        <mrow>du \amp = -\sin(x)\;dx \amp dv \amp = e^x\;dx</mrow>
                    </md>
                    <md>
                        <mrow> \int e^x\sin(x)\;dx \amp = e^x\sin(x) - \int e^x\cos(x)\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = e^x \sin(x) - e^x\cos(x) - \int e^x\sin(x)\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow>2\int e^x\sin(x)\;dx \amp = e^x\sin(x) - e^x\cos(x) </mrow>
                        <mrow>\int e^x\sin(x)\;dx \amp = \frac{e^x\sin(x) - e^x\cos(x)}{2} +C</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\dint \sin^{-1}(x)\;dx</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>u \amp = \sin^{-1}(x) \amp v \amp = x</mrow>
                        <mrow>du \amp = \frac{1}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}\;dx \amp dv \amp = dx</mrow>
                    </md>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\int \sin^{-1}(x)\;dx \amp = x\sin^{-1}(x) - \int \frac{x}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow>u \amp = 1-x^2 </mrow>
                        <mrow>du \amp = -2x\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow>\int \sin^{-1}(x)\;dx \amp = x\sin^{-1}(x) - \int \frac{x}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = x\sin^{-1}(x) + \frac{1}{2} \int u^{-1/2}\;du</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = x\sin^{-1}(x) + \sqrt{u}+C</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = x\sin^{-1}(x) + \sqrt{1-x^2} + C</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\dint \tan^{-1}(x)\;dx</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>u \amp = \tan^{-1}(x) \amp v \amp = x</mrow>
                        <mrow>du \amp = \frac{1}{x^2+1}\;dx \amp dv \amp = dx</mrow>
                    </md>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\int \tan^{-1}(x)\;dx \amp = x\tan^{-1}(x) - \int \frac{x}{x^2+1}\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow>u \amp = x^2+1 </mrow>
                        <mrow>du \amp = 2x\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow>\int \tan^{-1}(x)\;dx \amp = x\tan^{-1}(x) - \int \frac{x}{x^2+1}\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = x\tan^{-1}(x) - \frac{1}{2} \int \frac{1}{u}\;du</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = x\tan^{-1}(x) - \frac{1}{2}\ln|u|+C</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = x\tan^{-1}(x) - \frac{1}{2} \ln(x^2+1) + C</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Evaluate the following definite integrals.
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\dint_{x=1}^{x=e} x\ln(x)\;dx</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>u \amp = \ln(x) \amp v \amp = \frac{x^2}{2} </mrow>
                        <mrow>du \amp = \frac{1}{x}\;dx \amp  dv \amp = x\;dx</mrow>
                    </md>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\int_{x=1}^{x=e} x\ln(x)\;dx \amp = \left(\frac{x^2\ln(x)}{2}\right)\bigg|_{x=1}^{x=e} - \frac{1}{2} \int_{x=1}^{x=e} x\;dx  </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \left(\frac{x^2\ln(x)}{2} - \frac{x^2}{4}\right)\bigg|_{x=1}^{x=e}</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \left(\frac{e^2}{2} - \frac{e^2}{4}\right) - \left(0 - \frac{1}{4}\right)</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \frac{e^2+1}{4}</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\dint_{x=0}^{x=\pi/4} x\cos(2x)\;dx</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>u \amp = x \amp v \amp = \frac{\sin(2x)}{2}</mrow>
                        <mrow>du \amp = dx \amp dv \amp = \cos(2x)\;dx</mrow>
                    </md>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\int_{x=0}^{x=\pi/4} x\cos(2x)\;dx \amp = \left(\frac{x\sin(2)}{2}\right)\bigg|_{x=0}^{x=\pi/4} - \frac{1}{2}\int_{x=0}^{x=\pi/4} \sin(2x)\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \left( \frac{x\sin(2x)}{2} + \frac{\cos(2x)}{4}\right)\bigg|_{x=0}^{x=\pi/4}</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \frac{\pi}{8} - \frac{1}{4} </mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\dint_{x=0}^{x=\ln(5)} xe^{x}\;dx</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>u \amp = x \amp v \amp e^x</mrow>
                        <mrow>du \amp dx \amp dv \amp e^x\;dx</mrow>
                    </md>
                    <md>
                        <mrow> \int_{x=0}^{x=\ln(5)} xe^x\;dx \amp = \left( xe^x \right)\bigg_{x=0}^{x=\ln(5)} - \int_{x=0}^{x=\ln(5)} e^x\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \left(xe^x - e^x \right) \bigg|_{x=0}^{x=\ln(5)}</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = (5\ln(5) - 5) - (0 - 1)</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = 5\ln(5) - 4</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                In this problem, we'll consider the integral <m>\dint \sin^2(x)\;dx</m>. We'll integrate this in two different ways!
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    We know that:
                    <md>
                        \dint \sin^2(x)\;dx = \dint \sin(x)\sin(x)\;dx
                    </md>. 
                    Use the Integration by Parts strategy, and especially note that you can solve for the integral (<xref ref="subsec-SolvingForIntegral" text="title"/>).
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\int \sin^2(x)\;dx \amp = -\sin(x)\cos(x) + \int \cos^2(x)\;dx </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = -\sin(x)\cos(x) + \int (1 - \sin^2(x))\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = -\sin(x)\cos(x) + \int 1 \;dx - \int \sin^2(x)\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow>2\int \sin^2(x)\;dx \amp = -\sin(x)\cos(x) + x</mrow>
                        <mrow>\int \sin^2(x)\;dx \amp = -\frac{\sin(x)\cos(x)+x}{2} + C</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    We can use a trigonometric identity to rewrite the integral:
                    <md>
                        \sin^2(x) = \dfrac{1-\cos(2x)}{2}
                    </md>.
                    So we have:
                    <md>
                        \int \sin^2(x)\;dx = \int \frac{1}{2} - \frac{1}{2}\cos(2x)\;dx
                    </md>.
                    Use <m>u</m>-substitution.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    We will let <m>u = 2x</m>, giving <m>du = 2\;dx</m>.
                    <md>
                        <mrow> \int \sin^2(x)\;dx amp= \int \frac{1}{2} - \frac{1}{2}\cos(2x)\;dx </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \frac{x}{2} - \frac{1}{4}\int \cos(u)\;du</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \frac{x}{2} - \frac{\sin(2x)}{4} + C</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Were your answers the same or different? Should they be the same? Why or why not? Are they connected somehow? 
                </p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>
                    They might be different, but they should only be different by, at most, a constant.
                </p>
            </hint>
        </task>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                For these next problems, we'll use <m>x=u^2</m> and <m>dx = 2u\;du</m> to substitute into the integral as written. Then use Integration by Parts.
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\dint \sin(\sqrt{x})\;dx</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\int \sin(\sqrt{x})\;dx \amp = \int 2u\sin(u)\;du</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = -2u\cos(u) + 2\int \cos(u)\;du </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = -2u\cos(u) + 2\sin(u) + C</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = -2\sqrt{x}\cos(\sqrt{x}) + 2\sin(\sqrt{x}) + C</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\dint e^{\sqrt{x}}\;dx</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\int e^{\sqrt{x}}\;dx \amp = \int 2ue^u\;du</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = 2ue^{u} - 2\int e^u \;du</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = 2ue^u - 2e^u + C</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = 2\sqrt{x}e^{\sqrt{x}} - 2e^{\sqrt{x}} + C</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                In <xref ref="ex-IntegralLog"/>, we found that
                <md>
                    \int \ln(x)\;dx = x\ln(x)-x+C
                </md>.
                Let's find this result again, but in a different way.                
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    We'll start with a <m>u</m>-substitution. Let <m>u=\ln(x)</m>. Solve for <m>x</m>, and find a substitution for <m>dx</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>x \amp = e^u</mrow>
                        <mrow> dx\amp = e^u\;du </mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Apply your substitution above. You should have an integral for which you can use integration by parts! Integrate.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow> \int \ln(x)\;dx \amp = \int ue^u \;du</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = ue^u-\int e^u \;du</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = ue^u - e^u+C</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = x\ln(x)-x+C</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use this same technique to integrate the following:
                    <md>
                        \int \left(\ln(x)\right)^3\;dx
                    </md>
                    
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>x \amp = e^u</mrow>
                        <mrow>dx \amp = e^u\;du</mrow>
                    </md>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\int \left(\ln(x)\right)^3\;dx \amp = \int u^3e^u\;du</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = u^3e^u - \int 3u^2 e^u\;du</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = u^3e^u - 3u^2e^u + \int 6ue^u \; du</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = u^3e^u - 3u^2e^u + 6ue^u - \int 6e^u\;du</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = u^3e^u - 3u^2e^u + 6ue^u - 6e^u + C </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = x(\ln(x))^3 - 3x(\ln(x))^2 + 6x\ln(x) - 6x + C</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>
</exercises>